window.__DOCS_INDEX__=[{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"Overview","u":"./","x":"What the library is and what one call gives you."},{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"What is in the box","u":"./#features","x":""},{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"How it works","u":"./#how-it-works","x":""},{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"Advanced","u":"./#advanced","x":""},{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"Never built a REST API before? Start here.","u":"./#basics","x":"Never built a REST API before? Start here. Skip ahead if router.get('/:id', …) is muscle memory for you. The paperwork analogy. Every office form is different, but the clerk's job is always the same: check the form is filled in correctly, file it, find it again later, amend it, shred it. Your models are the forms. The clerk's job — read, create, update, delete — is code you would otherwise write again for every single model, and get subtly wrong in a different way each time. This library is the clerk. You hand it a model and a list of house rules, and it handles the paperwork identically every"},{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"Words you'll see on every page","u":"./#words-you-ll-see-on-every-page","x":"Words you'll see on every page# Word What it actually means Model A Mongoose model — the shape of one kind of record, like Product or User. It's the only required option. Router A bundle of URL routes you attach to your Express app with app.use('/api/products', router). Controller The functions that actually answer a request. This library generates them for you; the class is called ControllerSets. CRUD Create, Read, Update, Delete — the four things every API does. Middleware A function that runs before your handler, typically to check who the caller is. Yours to supply, via the middlewares opt"},{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"What you actually get","u":"./#what-you-get","x":"What you actually get Three exports carry almost all the value. The rest is detail. createRouter() Six REST endpoints for one model, ready to mount — including HTTP QUERY. createRouterS3upload() The same six, plus file uploads to S3 on POST and PATCH. errorHandler One Express error handler that turns any failure into consistent JSON. That is the whole surface for most projects: import { createRouter, createRouterS3upload, errorHandler } from 'express-controller-sets'; app.use('/api/products', createRouter({ model: Product, /* rules */ })); app.use('/api/users', createRouterS3upload({ model: Us"},{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"A request, step by step","u":"./#lifecycle","x":"A request, step by step What happens between GET /api/products?s=laptop and the JSON that comes back. Your code Middlewares Auth, rate limits, tenancy Library Build filters Only allowlisted params Library Apply search Escaped, relations resolved Your hook onGet Choose populate & select MongoDB Query Capped at maxLimit Library JSON out Always the same shape A write (POST or PATCH) follows the same path with two differences: on an upload router the file middleware runs first and replaces the file with its URL, and the request body is filtered down to the fields you allowed before it reaches Mong"},{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"Nothing is open until you open it","u":"./#implicit-deny","x":"Nothing is open until you open it One idea explains almost every option in this library. Learn it once and the rest of the page reads itself. The trap \"It works out of the box\" A generated API that exposes everything by default is convenient on day one and a breach on day ninety. Someone filters on passwordResetToken, or POSTs {\"role\":\"admin\"}, and nothing stops them. The rule here Implicit deny A field is not filterable, not sortable and not writable until you name it. Reads are capped. Uploads are private. Every convenience is opt-in, in one line."},{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"What is closed, and the key that opens it","u":"./#what-is-closed-and-the-key-that-opens-it","x":"What is closed, and the key that opens it# A client wants to… Option Default if you say nothing Filter by a field query Nothing is filterable Compare or range a field, or name it in a QUERY filter filterableFields Falls back to query Sort by a field sortableFields Falls back to query + your orderBy field Search text search Search does nothing Write a field allowedFields / blockedFields Everything writable — plus a startup warning Read with a body instead of a URL enableQuery Mounted — but its filter and sort still obey the two allowlists above, even in legacyMode Read the whole collection maxL"},{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"Fields no configuration can unlock","u":"./#fields-no-configuration-can-unlock","x":"Fields no configuration can unlock# Regardless of what you allow, these are stripped from every request body — they are database bookkeeping or MongoDB syntax, never client data: _id __v createdAt updatedAt any $-prefixed key any key containing . __proto__ constructor prototype Why $ and . matter. To MongoDB they are not characters, they are syntax: $ starts an operator and . reaches into a subdocument. A body of {\"$where\": \"…\"} or {\"owner.role\": \"admin\"} is an instruction, not data. The check runs on nested values too, up to eight levels deep — beyond that the value is refused rather than vou"},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"Quickstart","u":"quickstart.html","x":"A working API in one file, in five minutes."},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"The whole thing in one file","u":"quickstart.html#one-file","x":""},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"Next steps","u":"quickstart.html#next","x":""},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"Where next","u":"quickstart.html#next","x":""},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"Where next","u":"quickstart.html#next","x":""},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"Advanced","u":"quickstart.html#advanced","x":""},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"Install options and requirements","u":"quickstart.html#install","x":"Install options and requirements One command for CRUD. A second one only if you want uploads. npm install express-controller-sets mongoose express Copy Uploads need two more packages. They are optional peers — the library imports them lazily, so if you never create an upload router you never pay for them: npm install multer @aws-sdk/client-s3 # optional: image compression before upload npm install sharp Copy Node 18 or newer Tested on Node 18, 20 and 22 on every commit. ESM only Use import, and add \"type\": \"module\" to your package.json. Types built in No @types/… package to install. Autocomple"},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"Your first API, end to end","u":"quickstart.html#first-api","x":""},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"Step-by-step walkthrough","u":"quickstart.html#first-api","x":""},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"Before you go live","u":"quickstart.html#security","x":"Before you go live Nine checks. None of them takes more than a minute. Set allowedFields On every router. If the startup console is clean, you've done it. Add authentication Nothing is protected until you supply middlewares. Keep the allowlists tight Only fields you would happily publish belong in filterableFields and sortableFields. Bound the slow reads Set maxTimeMS, and maxPage if the collection is large. ?page=9999999 is a cheap request that makes the database walk every skipped index entry; these are what stop it. Check maxLimit 100 by default. Raise it only as far as your slowest collect"},{"p":"How to use","g":"Get started","t":"How to use","u":"how-to-use.html","x":"Put CRUD, auth, uploads and email together — plus ready-made answers to common tasks."},{"p":"How to use","g":"Get started","t":"The building blocks","u":"how-to-use.html#building-blocks","x":""},{"p":"How to use","g":"Get started","t":"All together","u":"how-to-use.html#full-app","x":""},{"p":"How to use","g":"Get started","t":"Common tasks","u":"how-to-use.html#common-tasks","x":""},{"p":"How to use","g":"Get started","t":"Public, read-only","u":"how-to-use.html#public-read-only","x":"Public, read-only An empty allowedFields means no field is writable, so POST and PATCH are refused with a 400. Add a guard if you also want DELETE gone. app.use('/api/articles', createRouter({ model: Article, orderBy: '-publishedAt', query: ['category'], search: ['title', 'summary'], allowedFields: [], // nothing is writable middlewares: [(req, res, next) => // only reads get through ['GET', 'HEAD', 'QUERY'].includes(req.method) ? next() : res.status(405).end()], })); Two routers, one model The most common shape: the public reads, staff edit. Each router carries its own allowlists, so the publ"},{"p":"How to use","g":"Get started","t":"Two routers, one model","u":"how-to-use.html#public-read-admin-write","x":"Two routers, one model The most common shape: the public reads, staff edit. Each router carries its own allowlists, so the public one can never write — even if a guard is misconfigured. const catalogue = { model: Product, orderBy: '-createdAt', query: ['category', 'isFeatured'], search: ['name', 'description'], filterableFields: ['price', 'category'], sortableFields: ['price', 'name', 'createdAt'], }; // Anyone: read app.use('/api/products', createRouter({ ...catalogue, allowedFields: [] })); // Staff: read and write app.use('/api/admin/products', createRouter({ ...catalogue, middlewares: [aut"},{"p":"How to use","g":"Get started","t":"Server-owned fields","u":"how-to-use.html#owner-from-token","x":"Server-owned fields Fields like author, ownerId or tenantId come from the signed-in user, never from the body. Keep them out of allowedFields and set them in validate.create — whatever the client sent for them was already stripped. app.use('/api/posts', createRouter({ model: Post, middlewares: [auth.requireAuth], allowedFields: ['title', 'body', 'tags'], // no 'author' validate: { create: (payload, { req }) => ({ ...payload, author: req.auth.userId }), }, })); Only my records Three pieces: force the owner into every list, check ownership on every /:id, and stamp the owner on create. Tested end"},{"p":"How to use","g":"Get started","t":"Only my records","u":"how-to-use.html#only-my-records","x":"Only my records Three pieces: force the owner into every list, check ownership on every /:id, and stamp the owner on create. Tested end to end — a user cannot list, read, change or delete another user's records, even by sending ?ownerId= themselves. import express from 'express'; import mongoose from 'mongoose'; import { createRouter, HttpError } from 'express-controller-sets'; // 1. Lists: pin ownerId to the caller. Express 5 re-parses req.query on every // read, so assigning to it does nothing — shadow it with a real property. const onlyMine = (req, res, next) => { Object.defineProperty(req,"},{"p":"How to use","g":"Get started","t":"Populate & search a ref","u":"how-to-use.html#populate-and-search","x":"Populate & search a ref onGet decides, per request, what to populate and select. Search can reach into a referenced model with a dotted path. app.use('/api/books', createRouter({ model: Book, // author: { type: ObjectId, ref: 'Author' } search: ['title', 'author.name'], // ?s=tolkien matches the author's name allowedFields: [], onGet: (req) => ({ populates: [{ path: 'author', select: 'name avatar' }], selects: req.query.full ? '' : '-body', // lists skip the heavy field }), })); Validate & normalise Throw ValidationError with a field map and the client gets it back as fields. Return an object "},{"p":"How to use","g":"Get started","t":"Validate & normalise","u":"how-to-use.html#validate-input","x":"Validate & normalise Throw ValidationError with a field map and the client gets it back as fields. Return an object to replace the payload — that is how you trim, lowercase or derive values. import { createRouter, ValidationError } from 'express-controller-sets'; const check = (p) => { const fields = {}; if (p.price !== undefined && !(p.price >= 0)) fields.price = 'must be zero or more'; if (p.sku !== undefined && !/^[A-Z0-9-]{4,20}$/.test(p.sku)) fields.sku = 'use 4–20 letters, digits or dashes'; if (Object.keys(fields).length) throw new ValidationError('Check the submitted values.', fields);"},{"p":"How to use","g":"Get started","t":"Large collections","u":"how-to-use.html#large-collections","x":"Large collections Offset pages get slower the deeper you go, and counting every match costs a full scan. Cursor pagination is one index seek per page at any depth. Index the sort field. // Schema: eventSchema.index({ createdAt: -1 }); app.use('/api/events', createRouter({ model: Event, orderBy: '-createdAt', pagination: 'cursor', // follow pagination.nextCursor: ?cursor=… maxTimeMS: 2000, // a slow query is cut off, not left running allowedFields: [], })); If you need page numbers but not totals, keep offset pagination and set countStrategy: 'none' — responses carry hasMore instead of totalPag"},{"p":"How to use","g":"Get started","t":"Image uploads to S3","u":"how-to-use.html#image-uploads","x":"Image uploads to S3 Install multer, @aws-sdk/client-s3 and (for compression) sharp, and set the S3 variables. Clients send multipart/form-data with the file fields named as below; the stored document gets the URLs. import { createRouterS3upload } from 'express-controller-sets'; app.use('/api/admin/products', createRouterS3upload({ model: Product, middlewares: [auth.requireAuth, auth.requireRole('admin')], path: 'products/', fields: [ { name: 'cover', maxCount: 1 }, { name: 'gallery', maxCount: 6 }, ], imgOptimizations: 'medium', upload: { acl: 'public-read', // product photos are meant to be s"},{"p":"How to use","g":"Get started","t":"Complete auth","u":"how-to-use.html#full-auth","x":"Complete auth Email and password, short access tokens with rotating refresh tokens, password reset by email, and a welcome mail. Add the fields from What your model needs to your user schema. import nodemailer from 'nodemailer'; import { createAuthRouter } from 'express-controller-sets'; const transporter = nodemailer.createTransport(process.env.SMTP_URL); export const auth = createAuthRouter({ model: User, identifiers: ['email'], token: { secret: process.env.JWT_SECRET, expiresIn: '15m' }, refresh: { enabled: true, rotate: true, expiresIn: '30d' }, roles: { list: ['user', 'admin'], default: '"},{"p":"How to use","g":"Get started","t":"Rate-limit sign-in","u":"how-to-use.html#rate-limit","x":"Rate-limit sign-in Accounts lock after repeated failures, but that doesn't stop someone trying one password against many accounts. Put a limiter on the routes that get guessed at — it runs before the database is touched. import rateLimit from 'express-rate-limit'; const tight = rateLimit({ windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, limit: 20 }); createAuthRouter({ // … middlewares: { login: [tight], register: [tight], forgotPassword: [tight], resetPassword: [tight] }, }); Extra endpoints A generated router is an ordinary Express router. Mount your own routes on the same path — before it, so they win. const pro"},{"p":"How to use","g":"Get started","t":"Extra endpoints","u":"how-to-use.html#extra-endpoint","x":"Extra endpoints A generated router is an ordinary Express router. Mount your own routes on the same path — before it, so they win. const products = express.Router(); products.get('/stats', async (req, res) => { const [row] = await Product.aggregate([{ $group: { _id: null, avg: { $avg: '$price' }, n: { $sum: 1 } } }]); res.json({ success: true, data: row ?? { avg: 0, n: 0 } }); }); products.use(createRouter({ model: Product, allowedFields: [] })); app.use('/api/products', products); // GET /api/products/stats, plus the six generated routes To reuse the generated behaviour under a different verb"},{"p":"Endpoints & responses","g":"CRUD","t":"Endpoints & responses","u":"endpoints.html","x":"The six routes every router serves, and what they return."},{"p":"Endpoints & responses","g":"CRUD","t":"Six endpoints from one call","u":"endpoints.html#crud-basics","x":""},{"p":"Endpoints & responses","g":"CRUD","t":"What comes back","u":"endpoints.html#crud-responses","x":""},{"p":"Endpoints & responses","g":"CRUD","t":"Common options","u":"endpoints.html#crud-options","x":""},{"p":"Endpoints & responses","g":"CRUD","t":"Advanced","u":"endpoints.html#advanced","x":""},{"p":"Endpoints & responses","g":"CRUD","t":"The six endpoints","u":"endpoints.html#endpoints","x":"The six endpoints Identical for every model, whether you use createRouter or createRouterS3upload. Method Path What it does Success GET / List records. Filter, search, sort, paginate. 200 QUERY / The same list, described by a JSON body instead of a URL. Details. 200 POST / Create one record from the writable part of the body. 201 GET /:id Fetch a single record by its 24-character ObjectId. 200 PATCH /:id Partially update a record. Returns the updated document. 200 DELETE /:id Permanently delete a record. 200 Mount the router wherever you like — the paths above are relative to the mount point: "},{"p":"Endpoints & responses","g":"CRUD","t":"What \"update\" really does","u":"endpoints.html#what-update-really-does","x":"What \"update\" really does# The update is a single atomic query (findByIdAndUpdate), not read-then-write, so two simultaneous updates cannot clobber each other in the gap. Schema validators run with context: 'query', which is what makes custom validators that use this behave correctly on updates. A missing record returns 404 rather than creating one. Response shapes Four shapes, and success is always there to branch on. A QUERY read returns the same list and paginated shapes as GET /. Under countStrategy: 'none' the pagination block carries hasMore in place of totalPages and totalRecords; under"},{"p":"Endpoints & responses","g":"CRUD","t":"Response shapes","u":"endpoints.html#responses","x":"Response shapes Four shapes, and success is always there to branch on. A QUERY read returns the same list and paginated shapes as GET /. Under countStrategy: 'none' the pagination block carries hasMore in place of totalPages and totalRecords; under pagination: 'cursor' it carries hasMore and nextCursor. List Paginated Single Delete & error // GET /api/products { \"success\": true, \"data\": [ { \"_id\": \"65af…\", \"name\": \"Laptop\", \"price\": 1299 } ] } Copy // GET /api/products?page=2&pageSize=20 { \"success\": true, \"data\": [ /* … */ ], \"pagination\": { \"currentPage\": 2, \"pageSize\": 20, \"totalPages\": 7, "},{"p":"Endpoints & responses","g":"CRUD","t":"Shaping responses with onGet","u":"endpoints.html#on-get","x":"Shaping responses with onGet One hook that decides, per request, which relations to expand and which fields to return. Populating relations is expensive, and different callers need different amounts of data. A mobile list view wants names; an admin screen wants everything. onGet lets one router serve both. onGet: (req, res) => ({ populates: [], // Mongoose .populate() argument — string, object, or array selects: '', // Mongoose .select() argument — e.g. 'name price -_id' }) Copy Simple By role List vs detail createRouter({ model: Product, onGet: () => ({ populates: 'category', selects: 'name p"},{"p":"Endpoints & responses","g":"CRUD","t":"Options at a glance","u":"endpoints.html#options","x":"Options at a glance Both router factories take one object. Six keys are consumed by the router itself; everything else is passed straight through to the controller. createRouterS3upload({ // ---- router-level ---- middlewares: [requireAuth], // run before every route in this router enableQuery: true, // mount the HTTP QUERY route on / path: 'products/', // S3 key prefix (upload routers) fields: [{ name: 'image', maxCount: 1 }], // form fields (upload routers) imgOptimizations: 'medium', // compression level (upload routers) upload: { acl: 'private' }, // everything else S3 (upload routers) // "},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Filter, search & paginate","u":"filtering.html","x":"Let clients find records — only by the fields you allow."},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Let clients find things","u":"filtering.html#filter-basics","x":""},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Query parameters","u":"filtering.html#filter-params","x":""},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Big collections","u":"filtering.html#filter-big","x":""},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Advanced","u":"filtering.html#advanced","x":""},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Filtering","u":"filtering.html#filtering","x":"Filtering Three ways to narrow a list, each gated by an allowlist. Equality Range Compare Every name in query becomes a query-string parameter of the same name. createRouter({ model: Product, query: ['category', 'isFeatured'] }); // GET /api/products?category=65af…&isFeatured=true // → { category: '65af…', isFeatured: 'true' } // Repeat a parameter to match any of several values: // GET /api/products?category=65af…&category=65b0… // → { category: { $in: ['65af…', '65b0…'] } } Copy A parameter that isn't in query is ignored entirely — not an error, just no effect. A parameter whose value arrive"},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Search","u":"filtering.html#search","x":"Search One parameter, ?s= (or ?search=), across as many fields as you list — including fields on related models. createRouter({ model: Product, search: ['name', 'description', 'category.name'], }); // GET /api/products?s=wireless // matches products whose name OR description contains \"wireless\", // OR whose category's name contains \"wireless\" Copy"},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"How relational search works","u":"filtering.html#how-relational-search-works","x":"How relational search works# A field with a dot is split at the first dot. The part before it must be a field on your schema declared with ref; the rest is the path to search on the referenced model. The library looks up the matching documents there first, then filters your collection by their IDs — no aggregation pipeline required. category.name searches name on the Category model author.profile.name searches the nested profile.name on the Author model notARef.name clause dropped — the field has no ref What if no relation matches? You get zero results, not \"the search term was ignored\". Silen"},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Search terms are treated as text, not patterns","u":"filtering.html#search-terms-are-treated-as-text-not-patterns","x":"Search terms are treated as text, not patterns# Regex metacharacters in the term are escaped, so ?s=c++ searches for the literal string c++. Terms longer than maxSearchLength (128 characters) are rejected with 400. Why escaping is not optional. An unescaped search term is a program the client wrote and your database runs. ?s=(a+)+$ triggers catastrophic backtracking inside mongod — one GET request, one pegged database. If you genuinely need clients to pass patterns, allowRawRegex: true exists — put it behind authentication and never on a public endpoint."},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"The single-field shorthand","u":"filtering.html#the-single-field-shorthand","x":"The single-field shorthand# createRouter({ model: Post, search: 'title' }); // Still read from ?s= / ?search= — a string just means \"one field\". Copy Sorting & pagination Two knobs the client turns, both bounded by you."},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Sorting & pagination","u":"filtering.html#sorting-paging","x":"Sorting & pagination Two knobs the client turns, both bounded by you."},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Sorting","u":"filtering.html#sorting","x":"Sorting# createRouter({ model: Product, orderBy: '-createdAt', // your default: newest first sortableFields: ['price', 'name', 'createdAt'], // what clients may choose }); // GET /api/products?sort=price → cheapest first // GET /api/products?sort=-price → dearest first (the '-' means descending) // GET /api/products?sort=secretKey → 400 Field 'secretKey' is not sortable. Copy Your own orderBy is never checked against the allowlist — it's your decision, not the client's. Only a client-supplied ?sort= is validated. If you omit sortableFields, it defaults to everything in query plus whatever fiel"},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Pagination","u":"filtering.html#pagination","x":"Pagination# GET /api/products?page=2&pageSize=20 Copy Adding ?page= switches the response into paginated form, with a pagination block alongside the data: { \"success\": true, \"data\": [ /* … */ ], \"pagination\": { \"currentPage\": 2, \"pageSize\": 20, \"totalPages\": 7, \"totalRecords\": 132 } } Copy Situation How many documents come back No ?page Up to maxLimit — 100 by default. Never the whole collection. ?page=1, no pageSize 50 — the default page size. ?page=1&pageSize=20 20. ?page=1&pageSize=5000 Clamped to maxLimit. A client cannot enlarge its own page. ?page=abc or ?page=-3 Treated as page 1 rather"},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Paging past the point where offsets stop working","u":"filtering.html#paging-past-the-point-where-offsets-stop-working","x":"Paging past the point where offsets stop working# ?page=4000 does not jump to page 4,000. It asks MongoDB to walk every one of the 199,950 index entries before it, and throw them away. Add the countDocuments that produces the totals, and both halves of an offset page get slower as the collection grows. Keyset pagination asks a different question — \"everything after this record\" — which is one index seek whatever the depth, and needs no count: createRouter({ model: Product, pagination: 'cursor', sortableFields: ['price'] }); Copy # The first page takes no cursor GET /api/products?pageSize=50 # "},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Every query parameter","u":"filtering.html#query-reference","x":"Every query parameter Everything GET / understands. For the body form of the same read, see HTTP QUERY. Parameter Example Notes page ?page=2 Switches on the pagination block. Starts at 1; junk values become 1. pageSize ?pageSize=20 Default 50, clamped to maxLimit. s / search ?s=laptop Case-insensitive, matched literally, across the search fields. Max 128 characters. sort ?sort=-price Must be in sortableFields, else 400. - means descending. any field named in query ?category=65af… Equality filter. Repeat the parameter for an $in match. rangeField + range ?rangeField=price&range=10-100 Inclusive"},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Putting several together","u":"filtering.html#putting-several-together","x":"Putting several together# # featured laptops between 500 and 1500, cheapest first, second page of 20 GET /api/products ?isFeatured=true &s=laptop &rangeField=price&range=500-1500 &sort=price &page=2&pageSize=20 Copy Filters combine with AND. The search term is the one exception: it becomes an OR across your search fields, and that whole group is ANDed with everything else. The same request as a QUERY body — identical result, nothing in the URL: { \"filter\": { \"isFeatured\": true, \"price\": { \"gte\": 500, \"lte\": 1500 } }, \"search\": \"laptop\", \"sort\": \"price\", \"page\": 2, \"pageSize\": 20 } Copy Measure"},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Measured performance","u":"filtering.html#performance","x":"Measured performance What the library costs you, next to writing the same endpoint by hand and next to the established alternative. How these were produced. 100,000 documents in MongoDB, indexed on the sort key, Node 24 on one machine, every participant serving the same collection over loopback. 600 sequential requests after 150 warm-up requests; the median and the 95th percentile of the full round trip. Loopback removes network latency, which makes the differences below look larger than they will on your infrastructure — the ordering holds, the ratios shrink."},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Listing page 1 — 50 of 100,000, filtered and sorted","u":"filtering.html#listing-page-1-50-of-100-000-filtered-and-sorted","x":"Listing page 1 — 50 of 100,000, filtered and sorted# Participant Median p95 Throughput Hand-written Express + Mongoose (with totals) 5.52 ms 6.68 ms 179/s controller-sets (with totals) 5.45 ms 6.33 ms 179/s express-restify-mongoose + /count (with totals) 5.72 ms 6.79 ms 173/s express-restify-mongoose (no totals) 0.64 ms 0.82 ms 1,488/s controller-sets, countStrategy: 'none' 0.55 ms 0.79 ms 1,702/s controller-sets, pagination: 'cursor' 0.55 ms 0.73 ms 1,712/s Two groups, and the line between them is the count — not the library. A page with totals costs about 5.5 ms whoever builds it, because co"},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"Going deep — page 1,000 of the same collection","u":"filtering.html#going-deep-page-1-000-of-the-same-collection","x":"Going deep — page 1,000 of the same collection# Participant Median p95 Throughput Hand-written Express + Mongoose 12.61 ms 13.70 ms 78/s express-restify-mongoose 12.07 ms 12.91 ms 82/s controller-sets (offset) 12.73 ms 13.67 ms 78/s controller-sets (cursor) 0.86 ms 1.17 ms 1,096/s Every offset implementation lands in the same place, because they are all asking MongoDB to walk 49,950 index entries and discard them. That cost is the database's, not the framework's, and it keeps growing with the collection. Keyset pagination asks a different question, and answers it in the same time at page 1,000"},{"p":"Filter, search & paginate","g":"CRUD","t":"The other routes","u":"filtering.html#the-other-routes","x":"The other routes# Route Hand-written controller-sets express-restify-mongoose GET /:id 0.28 ms 0.27 ms 0.30 ms POST / 0.42 ms 0.39 ms 0.42 ms Within noise of each other, which is the point: the allowlists, the field policy and the response envelope are object operations on a payload that was already parsed. They do not show up next to a database round trip. What to take from this. The library is not faster than the code you would write by hand — it runs the same queries, and it measures like it. What it gives you is the faster strategy already built: countStrategy and pagination: 'cursor' are "},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"Create, update & validate","u":"writing.html","x":"Choose which fields clients may write, and check them before saving."},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"Say what clients may write","u":"writing.html#write-basics","x":""},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"Validate before saving","u":"writing.html#write-validate","x":""},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"Common options","u":"writing.html#write-options","x":""},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"Advanced","u":"writing.html#advanced","x":""},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"Create & update","u":"writing.html#writing","x":"Create & update The request body is filtered before Mongoose ever sees it. This section is the single most important one for security."},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"Say what a client may write","u":"writing.html#say-what-a-client-may-write","x":"Say what a client may write# // Allowlist — name what IS writable (recommended) createRouter({ model: User, allowedFields: ['name', 'email', 'avatar'], }); // Blocklist — name what is NOT writable createRouter({ model: User, blockedFields: ['role', 'isAdmin', 'balance'], }); // Per verb — stricter on update than on create createRouter({ model: User, allowedFields: { create: ['name', 'email', 'password'], update: ['name', 'avatar'], // email and password need their own flow }, }); Copy Both options may be combined; blockedFields is applied after allowedFields, so a field on both lists is blocke"},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"What a client sends vs. what gets saved","u":"writing.html#what-a-client-sends-vs-what-gets-saved","x":"What a client sends vs. what gets saved# // allowedFields: ['name', 'email'] // The client POSTs: { \"name\": \"Ayesha\", \"email\": \"ayesha@example.com\", \"role\": \"admin\", // ✘ not allowlisted → dropped \"_id\": \"65af…\", // ✘ always immutable → dropped \"$where\": \"1 === 1\" // ✘ Mongo operator syntax → dropped } // What reaches the database: { \"name\": \"Ayesha\", \"email\": \"ayesha@example.com\" } Copy Dropped fields are dropped quietly — the request still succeeds with the fields that survived. One exception: if nothing survives, you get 400 \"Request body contains no writable fields.\" rather than an empty r"},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"Validating writes","u":"writing.html#validation","x":"Validating writes Two gates stand between a request body and your collection, and they answer different questions. The field policy allowedFields decides what a client may set. A question of authority — not yours to delegate. Your validator validate decides whether the values make sense. A question only your domain can answer. The schema Mongoose's own validators run last, unchanged — types, required, enum, your custom paths. The order matters: your hook is handed the payload after the field policy has filtered it, so it never has to wonder whether role in front of it came from an admin or fro"},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"What the hook receives","u":"writing.html#what-the-hook-receives","x":"What the hook receives# Argument What it holds payload The body, already filtered to the fields this client may write. Never empty — an empty one is a 400 before the hook runs. context.req · context.res The Express request and response, for req.user, headers, uploaded files. context.operation 'create' or 'update' — useful when one function serves both. context.id The target document's id on an update; undefined on a create. context.model The Mongoose model, so a uniqueness check needs no extra import."},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"What it may return, and what it may throw","u":"writing.html#what-it-may-return-and-what-it-may-throw","x":"What it may return, and what it may throw# The hook… Result returns nothing The payload is written as filtered. The common case. returns an object That object is written instead — your chance to normalise values or add server-owned fields. throws ValidationError 400 (or the status you pass) with a fields map. throws any HttpError That status, with your message. Use it for 403 and friends. throws anything else 500, with the detail kept in your log and out of the response. is async Awaited. Query the database in it if the rule needs to."},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"The rejection a client sees","u":"writing.html#the-rejection-a-client-sees","x":"The rejection a client sees# // 400 Bad Request { \"success\": false, \"error\": \"Check the submitted values.\", \"fields\": { \"price\": \"must not be negative\" } } Copy fields appears only when there is one, so a client that never looks for it sees the shape it always has. Mongoose validation failures and duplicate-key conflicts fill it in too, which means one rendering path on the client covers every rejected write. It is never sent on a 5xx — an internal failure's detail is not the caller's business. Your hook runs before the schema, so required fields may be missing. That ordering is deliberate — i"},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"The after-create hook","u":"writing.html#after-create","x":"The after-create hook Run something once a record exists — send a welcome email, push a job onto a queue, warm a cache. createRouter({ model: User, allowedFields: ['name', 'email'], runAfterCreate: async (user) => { await sendWelcomeEmail(user.email); await queue.add('index-user', { id: user._id }); }, }); Copy The hook receives the created document and is awaited before the 201 goes out. Default Failures are logged, not raised If the email service is down, the user is still created and the client still gets 201. The record exists, so reporting failure would tell the client to retry a write th"},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"Errors & status codes","u":"writing.html#errors","x":"Errors & status codes Every failure comes back in the same shape. Mount the handler once and stop writing try/catch in route files. import { errorHandler } from 'express-controller-sets'; app.use('/api/products', productRouter); app.use('/api/users', userRouter); app.use(errorHandler); // AFTER all routes — Express requires this order Copy"},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"The error response","u":"writing.html#the-error-response","x":"The error response# { \"success\": false, \"error\": \"Validation Error: price is required\", \"requestId\": \"0f2a5b8c-…\" } Copy requestId is echoed into your server log alongside the full message and stack. When a user reports \"it said Internal Server Error\", that ID takes you straight to the line. A stack field is added only when NODE_ENV=development."},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"What each status code means","u":"writing.html#what-each-status-code-means","x":"What each status code means# Code When you'll see it Who should fix it 200 Successful read, update or delete. — 201 Record created. — 400 Malformed ID, a field that isn't filterable or sortable, an invalid filter value, a search term over 128 characters, a body with no writable fields, a schema validation failure, a rejected file type, a Multer limit, or an unknown key, operator or non-integer page in a QUERY body. The caller 404 No record with that ID — on read, update or delete. The caller 415 A QUERY body that isn't JSON, or one no mounted body parser handled. The caller, or you — the messa"},{"p":"Create, update & validate","g":"CRUD","t":"Duplicate keys are a 409, not a 500","u":"writing.html#duplicate-keys-are-a-409-not-a-500","x":"Duplicate keys are a 409, not a 500# { \"success\": false, \"error\": \"Duplicate value for: email.\", \"requestId\": \"…\" } Copy A unique-constraint collision is something the caller can fix by sending a different value, so it is reported as a conflict rather than a server fault. Your front end can show \"that email is already registered\" without parsing message text."},{"p":"Upload files","g":"S3 Upload","t":"Upload files","u":"uploads.html","x":"Accept files with a record and store them in S3-compatible storage."},{"p":"Upload files","g":"S3 Upload","t":"Upload files with a record","u":"uploads.html#upload-basics","x":""},{"p":"Upload files","g":"S3 Upload","t":"Common options","u":"uploads.html#upload-options-short","x":""},{"p":"Upload files","g":"S3 Upload","t":"Advanced","u":"uploads.html#advanced","x":""},{"p":"Upload files","g":"S3 Upload","t":"How uploads work","u":"uploads.html#uploads","x":"How uploads work Swap one function name and POST/PATCH start accepting files. import { createRouterS3upload } from 'express-controller-sets'; const userRouter = createRouterS3upload({ model: User, allowedFields: ['name', 'email', 'avatar'], // 'avatar' must be writable! path: 'avatars/', // key prefix inside the bucket fields: [{ name: 'avatar', maxCount: 1 }], // the multipart field name imgOptimizations: 'medium', // optional compression }); app.use('/api/users', userRouter); Copy From the browser, send multipart/form-data — regular fields and the file together: const form = new FormData(); "},{"p":"Upload files","g":"S3 Upload","t":"What happens to the file","u":"uploads.html#what-happens-to-the-file","x":"What happens to the file# Received into memory Multer buffers it — no temp files on disk. Size and count limits apply here. The bytes are identified The library reads the file's own signature. The Content-Type the client sent is ignored: it is a claim, and the bytes are the fact. An unrecognised file is rejected with 400. Checked against the allowlist Only the detected type is compared to allowedMimeTypes. Renaming payload.html to photo.png changes nothing. Optionally compressed JPEG, PNG and WebP are re-encoded if imgOptimizations is set. See Image optimization. Uploaded with a safe key and h"},{"p":"Upload files","g":"S3 Upload","t":"Which types are accepted, inline, or refused","u":"uploads.html#which-types-are-accepted-inline-or-refused","x":"Which types are accepted, inline, or refused# Type Allowed by default Served inline Notes JPEG, PNG, WebP ✔ ✔ Also the only formats that can be compressed. GIF, AVIF ✔ ✔ Stored untouched — never transcoded. PDF ✔ ✘ Downloaded rather than rendered. HTML, SVG, XML, JavaScript ✘ ✘ Active content. Even if you allowlist it, it is always attachment. MP4, HEIC, ZIP, MP3 ✘ ✘ Recognised, but you must allowlist them explicitly. Anything unrecognised ✘ ✘ 400. \"Unknown\" is not treated as \"probably fine\". Upload options Everything about files lives in three top-level keys plus the upload object. createRout"},{"p":"Upload files","g":"S3 Upload","t":"Upload options","u":"uploads.html#upload-options","x":"Upload options Everything about files lives in three top-level keys plus the upload object. createRouterS3upload({ model: Document, path: 'documents/', // key prefix fields: [ { name: 'file', maxCount: 1 }, { name: 'pages', maxCount: 10, formatToUrlObject: true }, ], imgOptimizations: 'high', upload: { acl: 'public-read', // opt in deliberately allowedMimeTypes: ['image/jpeg', 'image/png', 'application/pdf'], maxFileSize: 5 * 1024 * 1024, // 5 MB per file maxFiles: 5, allowClientImageOptions: false, }, }); Copy Option Default What it controls path 'files/' Key prefix inside the bucket. Normali"},{"p":"Upload files","g":"S3 Upload","t":"Image optimization","u":"uploads.html#images","x":"Image optimization Shrink images on the way to S3, without picking quality numbers yourself. Set a level and the library re-encodes the image toward a target size, binary-searching encoder quality (bounded between 25 and 95, at most seven attempts) until it lands in range. You get predictable output sizes instead of a fixed quality that ruins some photos and barely touches others. Level Target result Good for 'low' About 75–80% of the original size Photography, product shots — barely visible change. 'medium' / 'med' About 60–65% The usual choice for user avatars and content images. 'high' Unde"},{"p":"Upload files","g":"S3 Upload","t":"What is and isn't touched","u":"uploads.html#what-is-and-isn-t-touched","x":"What is and isn't touched# JPEG · PNG · WebP re-encoded in the same format GIF · AVIF · PDF uploaded exactly as received sharp missing or failing warning logged, original uploaded Why GIFs are left alone. An earlier version pushed every image through the JPEG encoder. A GIF came out as JPEG bytes stored under a .gif key with Content-Type: image/gif — animation gone, file corrupt, and nothing in the logs. Formats that cannot be re-encoded in place are now passed straight through, and the stored extension is re-derived from whatever bytes actually get uploaded. allowClientImageOptions is off for"},{"p":"S3 setup","g":"S3 Upload","t":"S3 setup","u":"s3.html","x":"Connect AWS S3, Cloudflare R2, DigitalOcean Spaces or MinIO."},{"p":"S3 setup","g":"S3 Upload","t":"Connect a bucket","u":"s3.html#s3-basics","x":""},{"p":"S3 setup","g":"S3 Upload","t":"Advanced","u":"s3.html#advanced","x":""},{"p":"S3 setup","g":"S3 Upload","t":"S3 environment","u":"s3.html#s3-env","x":"S3 environment Five variables. Only needed if you use an upload router. S3_ENDPOINT=https://nyc3.digitaloceanspaces.com # required S3_SPACES_KEY=your-access-key # required S3_SPACES_SECRET=your-secret-key # required S3_BUCKET_NAME=your-bucket # required S3_REGION=us-east-1 # optional, defaults to us-east-1 Copy The variable names carry \"SPACES\" because the client is configured with path-style addressing, which works with DigitalOcean Spaces, MinIO, Cloudflare R2 and Amazon S3 alike. Any S3-compatible endpoint will do. This library does not call dotenv.config(). Loading environment is your appl"},{"p":"Router & middlewares","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Router & middlewares","u":"router.html","x":"Mount, guard and split the generated routes."},{"p":"Router & middlewares","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"One call, all routes","u":"router.html#router-basics","x":""},{"p":"Router & middlewares","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Different rules for reads and writes","u":"router.html#router-split","x":""},{"p":"Router & middlewares","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Advanced","u":"router.html#advanced","x":""},{"p":"Router & middlewares","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Guards, tenancy and per-user data","u":"router.html#middlewares","x":"Guards, tenancy and per-user data The library builds the routes. Deciding who may call them is entirely yours. createRouter({ model: Order, middlewares: [requireAuth, requireRole('staff')], // run before every route allowedFields: ['status', 'note'], }); Copy Everything in middlewares is applied with router.use(), so it guards all five endpoints in that router. When different verbs need different rules, use two routers on the same path: // Public: anyone may read the catalogue app.use('/api/products', createRouter({ model: Product, query: ['category'], allowedFields: [], // no writes get throu"},{"p":"HTTP QUERY","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"HTTP QUERY","u":"http-query.html","x":"Reads whose filters travel in a JSON body."},{"p":"HTTP QUERY","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"A read with a JSON body","u":"http-query.html#query-basics","x":""},{"p":"HTTP QUERY","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Advanced","u":"http-query.html#advanced","x":""},{"p":"HTTP QUERY","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"The full HTTP QUERY guide","u":"http-query.html#http-query","x":"The full HTTP QUERY guide The same read as GET /, described by a JSON body instead of a URL — for filters that are too long, too structured, or too sensitive to put in a query string. QUERY is a real HTTP method (RFC 10008), and like GET it is safe and idempotent: it never writes anything. The difference is only where the parameters live. URLs run out Proxies and servers cap request lines around 4–8 KB. Two hundred ids in an in list is not a URL. URLs get logged Query strings land in access logs, proxy logs, browser history and Referer headers. Bodies do not. Structure survives Two bounds on o"},{"p":"HTTP QUERY","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"A request","u":"http-query.html#a-request","x":"A request# QUERY /api/products HTTP/1.1 Content-Type: application/json { \"filter\": { \"category\": \"65af…\", \"price\": { \"gte\": 50, \"lte\": 250 }, \"tag\": [\"sale\", \"clearance\"] }, \"search\": \"office chair\", \"sort\": [\"-price\", \"name\"], \"page\": 1, \"pageSize\": 20 } Copy The response is exactly what the equivalent GET would have returned — same envelope, same pagination block, same onGet shaping, same maxLimit cap. Nothing about the read changes; only the request does. The body is not a MongoDB query. Operators are written without $ and looked up in a fixed table, so a body carrying $where or $function f"},{"p":"HTTP QUERY","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"What the body may contain","u":"http-query.html#what-the-body-may-contain","x":"What the body may contain# Key Type Meaning filter object Field → condition. Every field must be in filterableFields (which defaults to query). search string Same term, same fields and same escaping as ?s=. Capped by maxSearchLength. sort string · string[] \"price\" or \"-price\". A list sorts on several keys, up to five, each checked against sortableFields. page integer ≥ 1 Switches on the paginated response. Cannot be combined with limit. pageSize integer ≥ 1 Records per page, clamped to maxLimit. On its own it implies page: 1. limit integer ≥ 1 Caps an unpaginated read. Also clamped to maxLimit"},{"p":"HTTP QUERY","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Conditions","u":"http-query.html#conditions","x":"Conditions# A condition is one of three shapes. { \"filter\": { \"category\": \"65af…\", // scalar → equality \"tag\": [\"sale\", \"clearance\"], // list → in \"price\": { \"gte\": 50, \"lte\": 250 } // object → operators } } Copy Operator Takes Matches eqa valueEqual to it. nea valueNot equal to it. gt · gtea valueGreater than, or greater than or equal. lt · ltea valueLess than, or less than or equal. ina list, 1–100 valuesAny of them. nina list, 1–100 valuesNone of them. Values are strings, numbers, booleans or null. Mongoose casts them against your schema on the way in, so \"gte\": \"50\" on a Number field works"},{"p":"HTTP QUERY","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"The same read, both ways","u":"http-query.html#the-same-read-both-ways","x":"The same read, both ways# Query string QUERY body ?category=65af… { \"filter\": { \"category\": \"65af…\" } } ?tag=sale&tag=new { \"filter\": { \"tag\": [\"sale\", \"new\"] } } ?rangeField=price&range=10-100 { \"filter\": { \"price\": { \"gte\": 10, \"lte\": 100 } } } ?compareField=price&compareOperator=gt&compareValue=50 { \"filter\": { \"price\": { \"gt\": 50 } } } ?s=chair { \"search\": \"chair\" } ?sort=-price { \"sort\": \"-price\" } — not expressible { \"sort\": [\"category\", \"-price\"] } ?page=2&pageSize=20 { \"page\": 2, \"pageSize\": 20 } One body can also do what no query string can: two bounds and an exclusion on different fi"},{"p":"HTTP QUERY","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Calling it","u":"http-query.html#calling-it","x":"Calling it# fetch curl axios const res = await fetch('/api/products', { method: 'QUERY', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, body: JSON.stringify({ filter: { price: { gte: 50, lte: 250 } }, sort: '-price', pageSize: 20, }), }); const { data, pagination } = await res.json(); Copy Browsers and Node's fetch both send QUERY as written — it is a valid method token, not a special case. curl -X QUERY http://localhost:3000/api/products \\ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ -d '{\"filter\":{\"price\":{\"gte\":50}},\"sort\":\"-price\",\"pageSize\":20}' Copy Handy while developing: -X QUERY with -d is"},{"p":"HTTP QUERY","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Asking whether it's there","u":"http-query.html#asking-whether-it-s-there","x":"Asking whether it's there# A client does not have to guess. OPTIONS on the collection lists the methods, and RFC 10008's Accept-Query names the format the body must be in — this library sends it on OPTIONS and on every 415: OPTIONS /api/products HTTP/1.1 HTTP/1.1 200 OK Allow: GET, HEAD, POST, QUERY Accept-Query: application/json Copy If QUERY is missing from Allow, either the route was turned off, the runtime is too old, or something between the client and the app stripped it."},{"p":"HTTP QUERY","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"What gets refused","u":"http-query.html#what-gets-refused","x":"What gets refused# Request Status Why { \"filter\": { \"secretToken\": \"x\" } } 400 Not in filterableFields. Same rule as ?rangeField=. { \"filter\": { \"$where\": \"…\" } } 400 A $-prefixed or dotted key is never a field name. { \"filter\": { \"price\": { \"$gte\": 1 } } } 400 Operators are spelled gte, not $gte. { \"sort\": \"createdBy\" } 400 Not in sortableFields. { \"filters\": { … } } 400 Unknown key. Ignoring it would mean returning everything. { \"page\": \"2\" } 400 JSON has numbers; page, pageSize and limit must be positive integers. Body sent as text/plain 415 A QUERY body must be JSON. JSON body, no parser m"},{"p":"HTTP QUERY","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Before you rely on it","u":"http-query.html#before-you-rely-on-it","x":"Before you rely on it# Node 22.2 or newer. An older runtime rejects QUERY inside its HTTP parser, before Express ever sees the request, and its Express router has no matching verb. The route is then skipped with a single warning rather than breaking the router — the rest of your API is unaffected. Check at runtime: import { isQueryMethodSupported } from 'express-controller-sets'; isQueryMethodSupported(); // → true on Node 22.2+ with Express 5 Copy What's in front of your app matters more than your app. Some proxies, CDNs, WAFs and API gateways pass only the methods they recognise, and a QUERY"},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Custom routes","u":"custom-routes.html","x":"Use the handlers on your own router."},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Use the handlers yourself","u":"custom-routes.html#custom-basics","x":""},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Advanced","u":"custom-routes.html#advanced","x":""},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Custom routes","u":"custom-routes.html#custom","x":"Custom routes When the six generated endpoints aren't quite the shape you need."},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Use the handlers on your own router","u":"custom-routes.html#use-the-handlers-on-your-own-router","x":"Use the handlers on your own router# Every controller method is a plain Express handler, so you can mount them individually — different middleware per verb, extra routes alongside, whatever you like. import express from 'express'; import { ControllerSets } from 'express-controller-sets'; import Product from './models/Product.js'; const controller = new ControllerSets({ model: Product, query: ['category'], allowedFields: ['name', 'price'], }); const router = express.Router(); router.get('/', controller.getAll); // public router.query('/', controller.query); // public, body-borne read router.get"},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"How the handlers are put together","u":"custom-routes.html#how-the-handlers-are-put-together","x":"How the handlers are put together# Each handler is a short composition of the same four steps, and every step lives in its own module under src/core/. Reach for them when you are writing an endpoint this library does not generate — a rule enforced in one of these applies to anything built on it. Module Responsibility core/config.js Options in, one frozen config out. Every default is resolved once, here. core/index.js The barrel everything outside core/ imports from — one import instead of nine. Modules inside core/ import each other directly, so the graph stays acyclic. core/getAll.js · query."},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Add routes to a generated router","u":"custom-routes.html#add-routes-to-a-generated-router","x":"Add routes to a generated router# A generated router is an ordinary Express router. Mount your own routes on the same path — declare the more specific ones first so they aren't captured by /:id. const extras = express.Router(); extras.get('/featured', listFeatured); // would otherwise hit /:id app.use('/api/products', extras); app.use('/api/products', createRouter({ model: Product })); Copy"},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"Uploads without CRUD","u":"custom-routes.html#uploads-without-crud","x":"Uploads without CRUD# import { fileUploadMiddleware } from 'express-controller-sets'; const upload = (req, res, next) => fileUploadMiddleware(req, res, next, { uploadPath: 'invoices/', fields: [{ name: 'invoice', maxCount: 1 }], allowedMimeTypes: ['application/pdf'], maxFileSize: 2 * 1024 * 1024, }); app.post('/api/invoices', requireAuth, upload, (req, res) => { res.json({ url: req.body.invoice }); // already uploaded and validated }); Copy TypeScript Type declarations ship with the package. Nothing to install. import express from 'express'; import { createRouter, errorHandler, type RouterOpti"},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Dynamic Router","t":"TypeScript","u":"custom-routes.html#typescript","x":"TypeScript Type declarations ship with the package. Nothing to install. import express from 'express'; import { createRouter, errorHandler, type RouterOptions } from 'express-controller-sets'; import Product, { type IProduct } from './models/Product.js'; const options: RouterOptions<IProduct> = { model: Product, orderBy: '-createdAt', query: ['category'], allowedFields: ['name', 'price'], onGet: async (req, res) => ({ populates: 'category', selects: 'name price' }), }; const app = express(); app.use('/api/products', createRouter(options)); app.use(errorHandler); Copy Exported types you'll reac"},{"p":"Auth setup","g":"Auth","t":"Auth setup","u":"auth.html","x":"Sign-up, login, roles and route guards on your own user model."},{"p":"Auth setup","g":"Auth","t":"Add sign-up and login","u":"auth.html#auth-basics","x":""},{"p":"Auth setup","g":"Auth","t":"Protect your routes","u":"auth.html#auth-protect","x":""},{"p":"Auth setup","g":"Auth","t":"What your model needs","u":"auth.html#auth-model","x":""},{"p":"Auth setup","g":"Auth","t":"Common options","u":"auth.html#auth-options-short","x":""},{"p":"Auth setup","g":"Auth","t":"Advanced","u":"auth.html#advanced","x":""},{"p":"Auth setup","g":"Auth","t":"All auth endpoints","u":"auth.html#auth-endpoints","x":"All auth endpoints Method Path What it does Who POST /register Creates an account and returns a token. Anyone POST /login Signs in with any configured identifier. Anyone POST /social/:provider google, apple, facebook, github. Anyone POST /password/forgot Sends a one-time code by email or SMS. Anyone POST /password/reset Verifies the code and sets a new password. Anyone POST /password/change Changes it, given the current one. Signed in POST /token/refresh Trades a refresh token for a new access token. Refresh tokens Anyone"},{"p":"Auth setup","g":"Auth","t":"A full configuration","u":"auth.html#auth","x":"A full configuration Register, sign in, social sign-in, password reset by one-time code, roles and user administration — against your own user model. This library defines no schema. import { createAuthRouter, createRouter, errorHandler } from 'express-controller-sets'; const auth = createAuthRouter({ model: User, identifiers: ['email', 'phone'], // sign in with either token: { secret: process.env.JWT_SECRET, expiresIn: '15m' }, roles: { list: ['user', 'staff', 'admin'], default: 'user', admin: ['admin'] }, registerFields: ['name'], updateFields: ['name'], // Short access tokens, long sessions."},{"p":"Auth setup","g":"Auth","t":"Your URLs, not ours","u":"auth.html#auth-urls","x":"Your URLs, not ours routes renames an endpoint or leaves it out, and middlewares behaves as it does on createRouter — with one addition: an object targets a single route by name. const auth = createAuthRouter({ model: User, token: { secret: process.env.JWT_SECRET }, routes: { register: '/signup', // rename it login: '/signin', social: false, // never mount it modifyRoles: false, }, middlewares: { all: [cors()], // every auth route login: [rateLimiter], // only the ones that get guessed at forgotPassword: [rateLimiter], }, }); app.use('/api/v1/auth', auth); Copy Route middleware runs before the"},{"p":"Auth setup","g":"Auth","t":"Signing in with anything","u":"auth.html#identifiers","x":"Signing in with anything One field, several identifiers. Each is normalized on the way in, so a phone number reaches the same account however it was typed: POST /auth/login { \"identifier\": \"Ada@Example.com\", \"password\": \"…\" } POST /auth/login { \"identifier\": \"+1 (555) 010-1234\", \"password\": \"…\" } # same account as POST /auth/login { \"identifier\": \"+15550101234\", \"password\": \"…\" } Copy Email is lowercased, a phone loses its formatting, anything else is trimmed. Supply your own with identifier('nationalId', (v) => v.toUpperCase()). What it refuses to do Attempt Result Registering with \"role\": \"a"},{"p":"Auth setup","g":"Auth","t":"What it refuses to do","u":"auth.html#auth-refuses","x":"What it refuses to do Attempt Result Registering with \"role\": \"admin\" The role is stripped. Roles have their own endpoint, behind admin. PATCH /users/:id with a new password or role Both ignored. Each has its own endpoint and its own authorization. Updating someone else's record 403, unless you administer. Telling a wrong password from an unknown account Same message, and a decoy hash is verified so the timing matches too. Guessing a password Counted on the record; the account locks. It survives a restart and holds across every instance. { \"password\": { \"$ne\": null } } 400. An object where a c"},{"p":"Refresh tokens","g":"Auth","t":"Refresh tokens","u":"auth-refresh.html","x":"Short access tokens, long sessions — with rotation on or off."},{"p":"Refresh tokens","g":"Auth","t":"Turn it on","u":"auth-refresh.html#refresh-enable","x":""},{"p":"Refresh tokens","g":"Auth","t":"What changes","u":"auth-refresh.html#refresh-endpoints","x":""},{"p":"Refresh tokens","g":"Auth","t":"Rotation: on or off","u":"auth-refresh.html#refresh-rotation","x":""},{"p":"Refresh tokens","g":"Auth","t":"Advanced","u":"auth-refresh.html#advanced","x":""},{"p":"Refresh tokens","g":"Auth","t":"Short tokens, long sessions","u":"auth-refresh.html#refresh-why","x":"Short tokens, long sessions An access token that lives for days is a stolen credential that lives for days. A refresh token lets the access token stay short — fifteen minutes — while the user stays signed in for weeks. The refresh token is stored server-side, so it can be revoked; the access token is checked by signature alone, so it is fast. ClientSign in ServerAccess token, 15 min ServerExpires → 401 ClientRefresh ServerNew tokens The client side Keep both tokens; on a 401, refresh once and retry. Store the refresh token you get back every time — it changes when rotating. Share one in-flight"},{"p":"Refresh tokens","g":"Auth","t":"The client side","u":"auth-refresh.html#refresh-client","x":"The client side Keep both tokens; on a 401, refresh once and retry. Store the refresh token you get back every time — it changes when rotating. Share one in-flight refresh between concurrent requests. let access = null; let refreshing = null; async function refresh() { refreshing ??= fetch('/api/auth/token/refresh', { method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, body: JSON.stringify({ refreshToken: localStorage.getItem('refreshToken') }), }) .then(async (res) => { if (!res.ok) throw new Error('signed out'); const { data } = await res.json(); access = data.token; localStorag"},{"p":"Refresh tokens","g":"Auth","t":"What it does on your behalf","u":"auth-refresh.html#refresh-security","x":"What it does on your behalf Tokens are opaque random values, not JWTs — only an HMAC of each is stored, so a leaked database holds no usable token. Password change and password reset revoke every refresh token. A stolen one does not outlive the password it was issued under. Sessions are capped at maxSessions (5) per user; the oldest is dropped, and expired ones are cleared on each sign-in. Every failure is the same 401 — unknown, expired, tampered or reused are not told apart. A refresh re-reads the user, so a role change or a disabled account takes effect at the next refresh, not when the old"},{"p":"Social sign-in","g":"Auth","t":"Social sign-in","u":"auth-social.html","x":"Google, Apple, Facebook, GitHub — or any provider."},{"p":"Social sign-in","g":"Auth","t":"Sign in with Google, Apple, Facebook or GitHub","u":"auth-social.html#social-basics","x":""},{"p":"Social sign-in","g":"Auth","t":"Advanced","u":"auth-social.html#advanced","x":""},{"p":"Social sign-in","g":"Auth","t":"Providers, and adding your own","u":"auth-social.html#social","x":"Providers, and adding your own Each provider is verified with the provider. A profile the client sends is a claim about who someone is, and none of these take one on trust. Provider Send How it is checked Google { idToken } RS256 signature against Google's published keys, then issuer and audience. Apple { idToken, name? } The same, against Apple's keys. Apple sends a name only on first authorization, so the client forwards it. Facebook { accessToken } debug_token confirms the token was minted for your app, then the profile is read. GitHub { accessToken } or { code } A code is exchanged server-"},{"p":"Email Sender","g":"Email Sender","t":"Email Sender","u":"email.html","x":"Send password-reset codes with your transporter, sender and templates."},{"p":"Email Sender","g":"Email Sender","t":"Send reset codes by email","u":"email.html#email-basics","x":""},{"p":"Email Sender","g":"Email Sender","t":"Common options","u":"email.html#email-options","x":""},{"p":"Email Sender","g":"Email Sender","t":"Swap the sender","u":"email.html#senders","x":""},{"p":"Email Sender","g":"Email Sender","t":"Custom templates","u":"email.html#templates","x":""},{"p":"Email Sender","g":"Email Sender","t":"Advanced","u":"email.html#advanced","x":""},{"p":"Email Sender","g":"Email Sender","t":"How password reset works","u":"email.html#password-reset","x":"How password reset works Three calls. The library generates the code, stores it safely and sends it through the channel you configured; your app only decides how mail and SMS leave the building. # 1. Ask for a code. The answer is the same whether or not the account exists. POST /auth/password/forgot { \"identifier\": \"ada@example.com\", \"channel\": \"email\" } → { \"success\": true, \"message\": \"If that account exists, a code has been sent.\" } # 2. The code arrives by email (or SMS), rendered from your template. # 3. Spend it. POST /auth/password/reset { \"identifier\": \"ada@example.com\", \"code\": \"402913"},{"p":"Email Sender","g":"Email Sender","t":"Transporter details","u":"email.html#mail","x":"Transporter details The default mail sender uses a transporter — a nodemailer transporter, or any object with sendMail({ from, to, subject, text, html }). Pass one in, or let the library build it from the environment. Pass a transporter From .env import nodemailer from 'nodemailer'; createAuthRouter({ model: User, token: { secret: process.env.JWT_SECRET }, appName: 'Acme', mail: { transporter: nodemailer.createTransport({ host: 'smtp.example.com', port: 587, auth: { user: process.env.SMTP_USER, pass: process.env.SMTP_PASS }, }), from: 'Acme <no-reply@acme.com>', }, }); npm install nodemailer #"},{"p":"Email Sender","g":"Email Sender","t":"Email or SMS: choosing a channel","u":"email.html#channels","x":"Email or SMS: choosing a channel The client may send \"channel\": \"email\" or \"sms\"; without it, email is used when configured, otherwise SMS. A channel you haven't configured is a 400 — decided before the account is looked up, so the answer is the same for every account. No address on file (a user without a phone asking for SMS) gets the same \"sent\" response as everyone else; the miss is logged, not revealed. The recipient is read from email and phone; change it with mail.toField / sms.toField. Upgrading from otp.deliver? It still works and overrides everything on this page. Move to mail / sms w"},{"p":".env examples","g":".env examples","t":".env examples","u":"env.html","x":"Every environment variable, with ready-made setups for common providers."},{"p":".env examples","g":".env examples","t":"How it works","u":"env.html#env-how","x":""},{"p":".env examples","g":".env examples","t":"Complete .env.example","u":"env.html#env-full","x":""},{"p":".env examples","g":".env examples","t":"CRUD only","u":"env.html#env-crud","x":""},{"p":".env examples","g":".env examples","t":"Auth","u":"env.html#env-auth","x":""},{"p":".env examples","g":".env examples","t":"Email sender","u":"env.html#env-email","x":""},{"p":".env examples","g":".env examples","t":"S3 upload","u":"env.html#env-s3","x":""},{"p":".env examples","g":".env examples","t":"Every variable","u":"env.html#env-reference","x":""},{"p":"All options","g":"Reference","t":"All options","u":"options.html","x":"Every option, default and export."},{"p":"All options","g":"Reference","t":"Every option","u":"options.html#option-reference","x":""},{"p":"All options","g":"Reference","t":"Controller options","u":"options.html#controller-options","x":"Controller options# Accepted by createRouter, createRouterS3upload and the ControllerSets constructor alike. Option Type Default Description model Model required The Mongoose model these endpoints operate on. orderBy string 'none' Default sort. Prefix with - for descending. Never allowlist-checked — it's your choice. query string[] [] Fields exposed as equality filters, and the fallback allowlist for filtering and sorting. search string | string[] [] Fields searched by ?s=. Dot-notation reaches into ref'd models. allowedFields string[] | {create, update} unset Fields a client may write. Unset "},{"p":"All options","g":"Reference","t":"Router-only options","u":"options.html#router-only-options","x":"Router-only options# Option Where Default Description middlewares both factories [] Express middlewares applied to every route in the router. enableQuery both factories true Mount QUERY / — see HTTP QUERY. Skipped automatically, with one warning, on runtimes that cannot parse the method. path S3 router 'files/' Key prefix inside the bucket. fields S3 router [{name:'file',maxCount:1}] Multipart file fields. imgOptimizations S3 router off 'low' | 'medium' | 'med' | 'high'. upload S3 router {} ACL, MIME allowlist and limits — see Upload options."},{"p":"All options","g":"Reference","t":"Exports","u":"options.html#exports","x":"Exports# Export Type Purpose createRouter(options) function Six CRUD endpoints for a model. createRouterS3upload(options) function The same, with S3 uploads on POST and PATCH. ControllerSets class The handlers themselves, for your own routes. errorHandler middleware Consistent JSON errors. Mount last. fileUploadMiddleware(req,res,next,options) middleware Uploads without the CRUD routes. compressImage(buffer,type,level) async Re-encode an image buffer toward a target size. HttpError class An error carrying a status whose message is safe to return. ValidationError class A rejected body, with opt"},{"p":"All options","g":"Reference","t":"Auth options","u":"options.html#auth-options","x":""},{"p":"FAQ","g":"Reference","t":"FAQ","u":"faq.html","x":"Answers to the questions people hit first."},{"p":"FAQ","g":"Reference","t":"Common questions","u":"faq.html#faq","x":""},{"p":"FAQ","g":"Reference","t":"Advanced","u":"faq.html#advanced","x":""},{"p":"FAQ","g":"Reference","t":"Upgrading from 2.x","u":"faq.html#upgrade","x":"Upgrading from 2.x 3.0 is a security release. Every breaking change is a default that used to be unsafe. If you run 2.x on a public endpoint, treat this as urgent. In 2.x a client could write any schema field, filter and sort on fields your API never returned, hang the database with a crafted search term, read entire collections in one request, and upload world-readable HTML to your bucket."},{"p":"FAQ","g":"Reference","t":"The fastest path","u":"faq.html#the-fastest-path","x":"The fastest path# createRouter({ model: Product, legacyMode: true }); Copy That restores 2.x behaviour so you can deploy today — then remove it one option at a time. legacyMode re-enables every vulnerability below and is scheduled for removal in 4.0. The full guide is in MIGRATION.md. What changed What to do Request bodies are filtered Add allowedFields or blockedFields to every router. ?compareField=, ?rangeField=, ?sort= are allowlisted List them in filterableFields / sortableFields, or rely on the query fallback. Search terms are escaped and length-capped Nothing, unless clients sent real p"},{"p":"FAQ","g":"Reference","t":"Constructor form","u":"faq.html#constructor-form","x":"Constructor form# // Still supported, deprecated new ControllerSets(Model, '-createdAt', ['category'], ['name']); // Preferred — and required for any 3.0 option new ControllerSets({ model: Model, orderBy: '-createdAt', query: ['category'] }); Copy"},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Reference","t":"Use with an LLM","u":"llm.html","x":"One prompt so ChatGPT, Claude or Copilot write correct code for this package."},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Reference","t":"Why a prompt?","u":"llm.html#why","x":""},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Reference","t":"Why a prompt?","u":"llm.html#why","x":""},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Reference","t":"The prompt","u":"llm.html#prompt","x":""},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Reference","t":"The prompt","u":"llm.html#prompt","x":""},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Reference","t":"Save it in your project","u":"llm.html#save-it","x":""},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Reference","t":"Advanced","u":"llm.html#advanced","x":""},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Reference","t":"Prompts that work well","u":"llm.html#ask","x":"Prompts that work well After the guide, ask in plain language. Say what the data is, who may read it and who may change it — the model turns that into allowlists and guards. Build an Express API for a blog with express-controller-sets: - Post { title, body, tags[], author (ref User), published } - Anyone can list and read published posts, search title and body, filter by tag, newest first - Only signed-in users can create posts; author is set from the token, never the body - Only admins can delete - Email + password auth with refresh tokens (rotation on), password reset by email via SMTP Give "},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Reference","t":"Check what it wrote","u":"llm.html#verify","x":"Check what it wrote Models are fluent, not infallible. Before running generated code, check these five things — they are where mistakes cost the most: Every router has allowedFields (or blockedFields) — and it does not include server-owned fields like role or ownerId. Every write route has a guard in middlewares, or is on a router with allowedFields: []. errorHandler is mounted last and express.json() first. Secrets come from the environment — token.secret is at least 32 characters and never in the code. Only real options are used. Anything not in All options is invented — at best ignored, at "}];
